  "semantic_notes": [
    {
      "heading": "[ edit 3 1 Quick Notes ]",
      "text": "3_1 is also known as \" The Trefoil Knot \", after plants of the genus Trifolium, which have compound trifoliate leaves, and as the \"Overhand Knot\". See also T(3,2) . [ edit 3 1 Further Notes and Views ] The trefoil is perhaps the easiest knot to find in \"nature\", and is topologically equivalent to the interlaced form of the common Christian and pagan \"triquetra\" symbol [12] : Logo of Caixa Geral de Depositos, Lisboa [1] A knot consists of two harts in Kolam [2] A basic form of the interlaced Triquetra; as a Christian symbol, it refers to the Trinity 3D depiction Further images... Trefoil/triquetra without outside corners (made from straight lines and 240° circular arcs) Triquetra made from circular arc ribbons Tightly-knotted form",
      "source": "rendered-text-window"
    },
    {
      "heading": "[ edit 3 1 Further Notes and Views ]",
      "text": "The trefoil is perhaps the easiest knot to find in \"nature\", and is topologically equivalent to the interlaced form of the common Christian and pagan \"triquetra\" symbol [12] : Logo of Caixa Geral de Depositos, Lisboa [1] A knot consists of two harts in Kolam [2] A basic form of the interlaced Triquetra; as a Christian symbol, it refers to the Trinity 3D depiction Further images... Trefoil/triquetra without outside corners (made from straight lines and 240° circular arcs) Triquetra made from circular arc ribbons Tightly-knotted form Celtic-style A Knotted Box [3]",
      "source": "rendered-text-window"
    },
    {
      "heading": "[ edit Notes on presentations of 3 1 ]",
      "text": "Knot 3_1. A graph, knot 3_1. A part of a knot and a part of a graph.",
      "source": "rendered-text-window"
